<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huallaga River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Huallaga is a river of central and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, about 1,140 km long, rising in the Andes near Cerro de Pasco and flowing north past Huánuco and through the San Martín region to join the Marañón. Its forests bordered the lands of the Chachapoya culture to the west. In the late 20th century the Upper Huallaga valley was a centre of coca growing and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a> insurgency.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huallaga-river.jpg" alt="Huallaga River" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: w:Flickr user Bruno Girin · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huallaga_River.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Huallaga is a tributary of the Marañón, part of the Amazon Basin, and rises on the slopes of the Andes in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. It joins the Marañón before that river reaches the Ucayali to form the Amazon. Its main tributaries are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monzon">Monzón</a>, Mayo, Biabo, Abiseo and Tocache rivers, and coca is grown in most of their valleys, which are exposed to periodic floods. Older names include Guallaga and Rio de los Motilones. Over nearly its whole length it is an impetuous torrent in a succession of gorges, with forty-two rapids, called pongos, and it crosses the Andes through the Pongo de Aguirre gorge. From there, 230 km from the Amazon, larger river boats can ascend to Yurimaguas in Loreto.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The river is divided loosely into three sections: the Upper Huallaga from the source to Tocache, the central Huallaga from Tocache or Juanjuí to Chazuta, and the lower Huallaga from Chazuta to Yurimaguas. Between the Huallaga and the Ucayali lies the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adikavi-pampa">Pampa</a> del Sacramento, a level region of alluvial land under dense forest about 480 km long and 40 to 100 km wide, first entered by Christian missionaries in 1726. Indigenous communities such as the Cocama-Cocamilla and Kichwa-Lamista still live there, with peasant farmers.</p><p>Since the 1980s the Upper Huallaga Valley has been the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s coca growing and drug trafficking. The coca was flown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, where it was made into cocaine. On 27 August 1994 a Drug Enforcement Administration aircraft crashed into a mountain north of Puerto Pizana in bad weather during a counter-narcotics flight, killing its five occupants, and the accident brought the end of the US anti-drug Operation Snowcap. In 1966 the actor Eric Fleming drowned in the river while a film was shot on location.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river and its banks suffer pollution from rubbish dumped in them. It supports a rich variety of wildlife and plants and is especially rich in amphibians, with eighteen species of frogs recorded.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huallaga River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chachapoya culture developed from around 800 in the montane forests between the Marañón and <b>Huallaga</b> rivers, in present-day Amazonas region of Peru.</blockquote><a href="/state/chachapoya">Chachapoya</a></li></ul></section>
